# June Development Update

It has been awhile since the last development update, therefor I will be
giving this update now!

There is a new CMake based build setup in the root of the project that
entirely replaces vendor-specific CI/CD installations, scripts, and otherwise.
This new system is used for the nightly builds on the main website! What this
means now is that SquirrelJME no longer depends on a specific service for its
builds and is now running on local hardware. This also means for you, that you
are now able to replicate the nightly builds from any revision you desire and
build it on your local system! This should work on Linux, macOS, and Windows!
The main motivation behind this was supply chain vulnerabilities in existing
CI/CD platforms such as GitHub Actions and service cost subsidization by
providers no longer being economically viable due to GenAI proliferation. A
benefit to running the builds locally, is that they build rather fast and
can be run more frequently.

Progress slowly continues on NanoCoat, currently the focus is on getting the
virtual machine in a running state. Then after that, the gauntlet that is
making sure it is fully working and stable.

A generic media player was added which allows you to play audio file formats
supported by SquirrelJME directly, even with the ability to rewind and
skip ahead! You can play files from any JAR and additionally there is built-in
capability to scan DoJa ScratchPads to find any MLDs or other media which
is built-in to those raw binaries.

Media Playback through Player has been greatly improved and refactored, all
Players now have a universal and compatible backing. Ones which already
existed, or were recently contributed, have been ported to the new system.

AShiningRay has contributed support for various Nokia, Samsung, and Sony
Ericsson audio formats. Additionally, they have implemented support for WAV
playback, including ADPCM and G.711! This means that most titles
which have non-Sun audio formats can now play sounds. Nokia sound support
includes Nokia OTA Ringtones as well.

ScritchAudio has been very slowly moving, however it still has many issues
to work out especially on Windows. Software Mixing works to support sound
cards which do not support simultaneous playback or specific audio formats.
On Windows, float playback is supported, however this unfortunately requires
at least Windows XP and your sound card to support this format.

MIME and file-type detection and guessing has been improved, however it is
limited to only the very start of the file for memory and performance reasons.

The Generic Connection Framework along with Connections has been refactored
greatly, which includes far greater support and handling for URIs in a more
generic way. All connection types, if not already, will be moving to this
generic platform as it handles URIs in an RFC compliant way and overall is
simpler to debug.

There have been minor DoJa fixes. A discrepancy where disposal was only
being performed for some images was fixed.

Optimizations with ProGuard have been refactored and fixed, considering this
is an eternal battle this may not be the last of this. As a part of this, it
was found that when using Standalone Hosted, it was mixing Release and Debug
which would result in crashes, odd behavior, or otherwise. However, it should
be noted that running Hosted is not supported anyway. Regardless, behavior
should be a bit more stable as ProGuard is now prevented from doing certain
actions.

Font Support has been improved, with my own self written algorithm I have
called the _Barcode Bitmap Font Scaling Algorithm_ or _Barcode Font Scaler_ for
short. This algorithm vertically scales any bitmap font up to a larger size
so that it may be used where a larger font is requested, however no such
font exists. Depending on the original font, it is readable up to double scale.
The algorithm prevents all "chunkiness" from occurring when upscaling fonts,
so that the fonts appear narrow. One limitation is that it does not perform any
horizontal scaling. It is quite interesting and novel however, only needing an
"Aha!" moment while staring at a wall!

Date tags were added all over the repository accordingly, as they have not
been added in quite a while.

Thread instances are now merged into a single object rather than being two
objects to represent Java threads and VM threads.

MEEP Cellular has had some of its interfaces defined, mostly for routing to
enable TODOs for future SMS support.

A Strict No-GenAI Policy for any kind of use was adopted, this does include a
reservation for a future project-approved fully-offline fully-local
OSI-approved SquirrelJME specific GenAI if such a tool were to exist in
the future. This policy was typed out manually and reviewed by neutral 
unaffiliated parties, with honest feedback and thoughts taken into 
consideration.

In the past six months, completion has progressed by 1% from 63% (176/287) to
64% (206/317). This may not seem like much increase, however there has been
a significant amount of splitting performed in the progress tracker. This is
ignoring the fact that the progress tracker was implemented only six months
ago.
